{
  "abstract": "A ten-chromatic biplanar graph must contain a 10-critical biplanar graph $G$ on at least twenty vertices. The order-twenty programme on the r/earth_moon forum fixes an independent triple $S$ and splits on the order $t$ of a 9-critical subgraph of $G-S$. We settle the branch $t=11$, in which this subgraph is $K_6\\vee C_5$. We prove that every $K_9$-free graph on 20 vertices with at most 108 edges and minimum degree at least 9 is 9-colourable whenever its vertex set splits into an independent triple $S$, six further vertices $T$, and an induced $K_6\\vee C_5$. The proof uses no independence-number or Gallai-forest arguments. An edge budget reduces the problem to list colouring of $(I_3\\vee K_6)-D$ with lists bounded below by the degree profile. Because $S$ is independent, the graph polynomial factors into elementary symmetric polynomials, which gives Combinatorial Nullstellensatz certificates for 1,062,973 of the 1,063,034 symmetry classes of maximal profiles. Every remaining configuration, namely the 61 residual classes and all 400 profiles they dominate, is refuted by an exact Hall-deficiency SAT encoding over the five 9-colourings of $K_6\\vee C_5$, with DRAT proofs checked by drat-trim. A stand-alone checker recomputes every certificate coefficient exactly and proves coverage: the profiles are pairwise inequivalent and match Burnside orbit counts. We also explain why the residuals exist. A Kostka-number argument gives an exact certificate criterion for the dense profiles, and every residual admits explicit lists of the guaranteed sizes, within nine colours, from which it is not colourable. So no list-size method can replace the core-colouring step. As a corollary, a 10-critical biplanar graph on 20 vertices has no eleven-vertex 9-critical subgraph after removing any independent set of size at least three. The branches $t=16,17$ remain open. The supplement contains all code, the certificates, logs and the residual data.",
